{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For the soul of the flesh, etc.</b> One needs to know the giving of a reason for why this is repeated. And according to what I explained above — they are the very same matters that we wrote — for it gives the reason why the soul that eats is cut off, inasmuch as the blood contains the soul and it imparts within it the bestial soul. Yet we still need to investigate why it said ״the soul of the flesh״ and did not say that the soul is in the blood. And it appears that HaShem employed wisdom to answer the questioner as to why He did not also forbid the flesh, for the vitality of the animal was also in it, and when a person eats it he would cause damage to his soul. Therefore it said: And was the soul of the animal in the flesh? It was not so; rather it was only in its blood, which is to say the blood of the flesh, and the blood is what gives life to the flesh. And the soul is not in the flesh at all, as it says, <b>the soul of the flesh is in the blood</b> — the soul is there and not in the flesh. And this disposition HaShem did not prepare in the disposition of man, for man — the extension of his vitality is both in the flesh and in the bones, and in their language it is called הבלא דגרמי, which remains after some years in the bones; and therefore the righteous do not decompose. And there are wicked ones who are likened to beasts — they are the ones who do not stand in the resurrection of the dead, inasmuch as their bones dried up and decomposed, and the soul expired and went its way like the spirit of the beast." "<b>And I have given it, etc.</b> Meaning: And you shall know that the blood is the soul, for I have given it to you, etc., to make atonement for your souls — a soul in place of a soul; behold, that the blood is the soul." "Further, the verse intends to warn concerning the soul of the animal, not to kill it, for HaShem did not give us the blood of animals except to make atonement for our souls. And this is what it says — <b>I have given it to you upon the altar</b> — to exclude any other purpose, for it is not given to us as a gift to do with it whatever we please to do. And you will find (Sanhedrin 2a) that the law of killing an animal requires that it be by a court of twenty-three, like the law of a person, for the verse equated their law to the law of a person. And an animal was not permitted for slaughter except for the needs of its flesh, when a person's soul craves to eat meat — for so the Purchaser of the world purchased and acquired for us, as it is written (Deuteronomy 12:20): ״כִּי תְאַוֶּה נַפְשְׁךָ לֶאֱכֹל בָּשָׂר בְּכָל אַוַּת״ etc."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The phrase הבלא דגרמי (literally 'vapor of the bones') is a kabbalistic/aramaic term retained in transliteration as it carries technical meaning; the Hebrew reads ונקרא בדבריהם הבלא דגרמי, 'and in their language it is called הבלא דגרמי.' I have kept the Aramaic untranslated."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:56.739604780Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}